.TH GETENV 3
.SH NAME
getenv \- value for environment name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
char *
getenv(name)
char *name;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B getenv
searches the environment, a list of strings of the form
.IR name = value
that a program receives from the one that ran it (see
.BR exec (2)),
for one with the given
.IR name .
It returns a pointer to the
.I value
part of that string, or NULL if there is none.
.PP
The whole environment is the array of strings
.BR "extern char **environ" ,
ended by a NULL pointer.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
	char *term;

	if ((term = getenv("TERM")) == NULL)
		term = "dumb";
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
sh(1), exec(2), environ(5)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B getenv
function first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX and 3BSD.
.SH BUGS
The value must not be changed through the pointer: it is the
environment itself, which a program run later inherits.
